  "result": "Attachment to and biofilm formation on abiotic surfaces by Acinetobacter baumannii: involvement of a novel chaperone-usher pili assembly system | Microbiology Society (https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.26541-0?crawler=true)\nciteturn44view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.26541-0?crawler=true\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 323\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 1887\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Skip to content】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Microbiology】 【3†Volume 149, Issue 12】\nL9: # Attachment to and biofilm formation on abiotic surfaces by Acinetobacter baumannii: involvement of a novel chaperone-usher pili assembly system 【4†Image: Free】\nL10: \nL11:   * 【5†Andrew P. Tomaras】^{1}, 【6†Caleb W. Dorsey】^{1,3}, 【7†Richard E. Edelmann】^{2} and 【8†Luis A. Actis】^{1}\nL12:   * 【9†View Affiliations Hide Affiliations】\nL13: \nL14: ^{1} 【10†Departments of Microbiology, Miami University, 40 Pearson Hall, Oxford, OH 45056, USA】 ^{2} 【11†Departments of Botany, Miami University, 40 Pearson Hall, Oxford, OH 45056, USA】\nL15: Correspondence Luis A. Actis\nL16:  【12†[email protected]†muohio.edu】\nL17: \nL18: 3 FNC1†Present address: Department of Medical Microbiology and Immunology, Texas A&M University Health Science Center, College Station, TX, USA.\nL19: \nL20:   * Published: 01 December 2003\nL21: \nL22:   * 【9†info Info】\nL23:     * 【13†Article information】\nL24:     * 【14†Full-Text】\nL25:     * 【15†Figures and Tables】\nL26:     * 【16†References (43)】\nL27:     * 【17†Cited By (626)】\nL28:     * 【18†Supplementary Material】\nL29:     * 【19†Metrics】\nL30: \nL31:   * 【9†build Tools】\nL32: ### Tools\nL33: \nL34:     * 【9†Add to my favourites】\nL35: \nL36:     * 【9†Create publication alert】\nL37: \nL38: 【9†Create citation alert】\nL39: \nL40: 【9†Create correction alert】\nL41: \nL42:     * 【9†Export citation】\nL43:       * 【20†BibT_{E}X】\nL44:       * 【21†Endnote】\nL45:       * 【20†Zotero】\nL46:       * 【22†Medlars】\nL47:       * 【23†RefWorks】\nL48:       * 【24†Mendeley†www.mendeley.com】\nL49:     * 【9†Recommend to library】\nL50:     *     * [Input]\nL51: ## Abstract\nL52: Acinetobacter baumannii causes severe infections in compromised patients, survives on abiotic surfaces in hospital environments and colonizes different medical devices. In this study the analysis of the processes involved in surface attachment and biofilm formation by the prototype strain 19606 was initiated. This strain attaches to and forms biofilm structures on plastic and glass surfaces, particularly at the liquid–air interface of cultures incubated stagnantly.\nL53: The cell aggregates, which contain cell stacks separated by water channels, formed under different culture conditions and were significantly enhanced under iron limitation. Electron and fluorescence microscopy showed that pili and exopolysaccharides are part of the cell aggregates formed by this strain.\nL54: Electron microscopy of two insertion derivatives deficient in attachment and biofilm formation revealed the disappearance of pili-like structures and DNA sequencing analysis showed that the transposon insertions interrupted genes with the highest similarity to hypothetical genes found in Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Pseudomonas putida and Vibrio parahaemolyticus.\nL55: Although the products of these genes, which have been named csuC and csuE, have no known functions, they are located within a polycistronic operon that includes four other genes, two of which encode proteins related to chaperones and ushers involved in pili assembly in other bacteria. Introduction of a copy of the csuE parental gene restored the adherence phenotype and the presence of pili on the cell surface of the csuE mutant, but not that of the csuC derivative.\nL56: These results demonstrate that the expression of a chaperone-usher secretion system, some of whose components appear to be acquired from unrelated sources, is required for pili formation and the concomitant attachment to plastic surfaces and the ensuing formation of biofilms by A. baumannii cells.\nL57:   * Received: 04/06/2003\nL58:   * Accepted: 05/09/2003\nL59:   * Revised: 27/08/2003\nL60:   * Published Online: 01/12/2003\nL61: \nL62: Keyword(s): 【25†DIP, 2,2′-dipyridyl】 , 【26†EDDHA, ethylenediamine-di-(o-hydroxyphenyl) acetic acid】 , 【27†GFP, green fluorescent protein】 , 【28†SEM, scanning electron microscopy】 and 【29†TEM, transmission electron microscopy】\nL63: \nL64: SGM\nL65: \nL66: 【30†Image: Loading】\nL67: \nL68: Article metrics loading...\nL69: \nL70: /content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.26541-0\nL71: \nL72: 2003-12-01\nL73: \nL74: 2026-10-07\nL75: ## Metrics\nL76: \nL77: 【30†Image: Loading full text...】\nL78: \nL79: Full text loading...\nL80: \nL81: /deliver/fulltext/micro/149/12/mic1493473.html?itemId=/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.26541-0&mimeType=html&fmt=ahah\nL82: \nL83: References\nL84: \nL85:   1. Barcak G. J., Chandler M. S., Redfield R. J., Tomb J. F. 1991; Genetic systems in Haemophilus influenzae . Methods Enzymol 204:321–342\nL86: 【31†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL87:   2. Baumann P., Doudoroff M., Stanier R. Y. 1968; A study of the Moraxella group II. Oxidase negative species (genus Acinetobacter ). J Bacteriol 95:1520–1541\nL88: \nL89: 【32†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL90:   3. Bergogne-Berenzin E., Towner K. J. 1996; Acinetobacter spp. as nosocomial pathogens: microbiological, clinical, and epidemiological features. Clin Microbiol Rev 9:148–165\nL91: 【33†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL92:   4. Bergogne-Berenzin E., Decre D., Joly-Guillou M. L. 1993; Opportunistic nosocomial multiply resistant bacterial infections – their treatment and prevention. J Antimicrob Chemother 32:39–47\nL93: 【34†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL94:   5. Bergogne-Berenzin E., Joly-Guillou M. L., Towner K. J. 19