omonas aeruginosa . Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A 97:4885–4890\nL153: \nL154: 【58†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL155:   30. Recht J., Martinez A., Torello S., Kolter R. 2000; Genetic analysis of sliding motility in Mycobacterium smegmatis . J Bacteriol 182:4348–4351\nL156: 【59†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL157:   31. Rosenberg M., Bayer E. A., Delarea J., Rosenberg E. 1982; Role of thin fimbriae in adherence and growth of Acinetobacter calcoaceticus RAG-1 on hexadecane. Appl Environ Microbiol 44:929–937\nL158: \nL159: 【60†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL160:   32. Sambrook J., Fritsch E. F., Maniatis T. 1989 Molecular Cloning: a Laboratory Manual , 2nd edn. Cold Spring Harbor, NY: Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory;\nL161:   33. Sauer F. G., Barnhart M., Choudhury D., Knight S. D., Waksman G., Hultgren S. J. 2000; Chaperone-assisted pilus assembly and bacterial attachment. Curr Opin Struct Biol 10:548–556\nL162: \nL163: 【61†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL164:   34. Solano C., Garcia B., Valle J., Berasain C., Ghigo J. M., Gamazo C., Lasa I. 2002; Genetic analysis of Salmonella enteritidis biofilm formation: critical role of cellulose. Mol Microbiol 43:793–808\nL165: 【62†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL166:   35. Stoodley P., Sauer K., Davies D. G., Costerton J. W. 2002; Biofilms as complex differentiated communities. Annu Rev Microbiol 56:187–209\nL167: \nL168: 【63†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL169:   36. Stover C. K., Pham X. Q., Erwin A. L. 28 other authors 2000; Complete genome sequence of Pseudomonas aeruginosa PA01, an opportunistic pathogen. Nature 406:959–964\nL170: 【64†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL171:   37. Thanassi D. G., Saulino E. T., Lombardo M. J., Roth R., Heuser J., Hultgren S. J. 1998; The PapC usher forms an oligomeric channel: implications for pilus biogenesis across the outer membrane. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A 95:3146–3151\nL172: \nL173: 【65†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL174:   38. Tolker-Nielsen T., Brinch U. C., Ragas P. C., Andersen J. B., Jacobsen C. S., Molin S. 2000; Development and dynamics of Pseudomonas sp. biofilms. J Bacteriol 182:6482–6489\nL175: 【66†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL176:   39. Towner K. J., Bergogne-Berenzin E., Fewson C. A. 1991; Acinetobacter portrait of a genus. In The Biology of Acinetobacter pp 1–24 Edited by Towner K. J., Bergogne-Berenzin E., Fewson C. A. New York: Plenum;\nL177: 【67†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\nL178:   40. Vallet I., Olson J. W., Lory S., Lazdunski A., Filloux A. 2001; The chaperone/usher pathways of Pseudomonas aeruginosa : identification of fimbrial gene clusters ( cup ) and their involvement in biofilm formation. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A 98:6911–6916\nL179: \nL180: 【68†[Google Scholar]†scholar.google.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Attachment to and biofilm formation on abiotic surfaces by Acinetobacter baumannii: involvement of a novel chaperone-usher pili assembly system (https://www.academia.edu/76955898/Attachment_to_and_biofilm_formation_on_abiotic_surfaces_by_Acinetobacter_baumannii_involvement_of_a_novel_chaperone_usher_pili_assembly_system)\nciteturn44view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.academia.edu/76955898/Attachment_to_and_biofilm_formation_on_abiotic_surfaces_by_Acinetobacter_baumannii_involvement_of_a_novel_chaperone_usher_pili_assembly_system\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 138\nL0: # Attachment to and biofilm formation on abiotic surfaces by Acinetobacter baumannii: involvement of a novel chaperone-usher pili assembly system\nL1: Microbiology (2003), 149, 3473–3484 DOI 10.1099/mic.0.26541-0 Attachment to and biofilm formation on abiotic surfaces by Acinetobacter baumannii: involvement of a novel chaperone-usher pili assembly system Andrew P. Tomaras,1 Caleb W. Dorsey,13 Richard E. Edelmann2 and Luis A. Actis1 Correspondence Departments of Microbiology1 and Botany2, Miami University, 40 Pearson Hall, Oxford, Luis A.\nL2: Actis OH 45056, USA 【0†[email protected]】 Acinetobacter baumannii causes severe infections in compromised patients, survives on abiotic surfaces in hospital environments and colonizes different medical devices. In this study the analysis of the processes involved in surface attachment and biofilm formation by the prototype strain 19606 was initiated. This strain attaches to and forms biofilm structures on plastic and glass surfaces, particularly at the liquid–air interface of cultures incubated stagnantly.\nL3: The cell aggregates, which contain cell stacks separated by water channels, formed under different culture conditions and were significantly enhanced under iron limitation. Electron and fluorescence microscopy showed that pili and exopolysaccharides are part of the cell aggregates formed by this strain.\nL4: Electron microscopy of two insertion derivatives deficient in attachment and biofilm formation revealed the disappearance of pili-like structures and DNA sequencing analysis showed that the transposon insertions interrupted genes with the highest similarity to hypothetical genes found in Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Pseudomonas putida and Vibrio parahaemolyticus.\nL5: Although the products of these genes, which have been named csuC and csuE, have no known functions, they are located within a polycistronic operon that includes four other genes, two of which encode proteins related to chaperones and ushers involved in pili assembly in other bacteria. Introduction of a copy of the csuE parental gene restored the adherence phenotype and the presence of pili on the cell surface of the csuE mutant, but not that of the csuC derivative.\nL6: These results demonstrate that the expression of a chaperone-usher secretion system, some of whose Received 4 June 2003 components appear to be acquired from unrelated sources, is required for pili formation and Revised 27 August 2003 the concomitant attachment to plastic surfaces and the ensuing formation of biofilms by Accepted 5 September 2003 A. baumannii cells.\nL7: INTRODUCTION include urinary tract infections, secondary meningitis, wound and burn infections, and particularly nosocomial Members of the genus Acinetobacter are non-motile, pneumonia (Bergogne-Berenzin et al., 1993, 1996).\nL8: Some ubiquitous Gram-negative bacteria that can be recovered of the challenges in the prevention and treatment of the from a wide range of sources such as soil, water, food infections caused by this opportunistic pathogen are its products and medical environments (Bergogne-Berenzin & remarkable widespread resistance to different antibiotics Towner, 1996). The latter source is of particular significance and its ability to persist in nosocomial environments and because A.\nL9: baumannii is the Acinetobacter genomic species medical devices (Bergogne-Berenzin & Towner, 1996). A. of greatest importance in human medicine. A large number baumannii survives for several days on inanimate objects of reports describe outbreaks of nosocomial infections that and surfaces found normally in medical environments, even in dry conditions on dust particles.\nL10: These survival 3Present address: Department of Medical Microbiology and Immuno- properties most likely play a significant role in the outbreaks logy, Texas A&M University Health Science Center, College Station, caused by this pathogen. TX, USA. Abbreviations: DIP, 2,29-dipyridyl; EDDHA, ethylenediamine-di-(o- The potential ability of A.\nL11: baumannii to form biofilms could hydroxyphenyl) acetic acid; GFP, green fluorescent protein; SEM, explain its outstanding antibiotic resistance and survival scanning electron microscopy; TEM, transmission electron microscopy. properties. This possibility is supported by a very limited The GenBank accession number for the sequence reported in this number of publications which showed that a clinical isolate paper is AY241696.\nL12: o